it's a fact that a lot of people end up  in the same jobs that their parents have  and I think it's a lot to do with things  like conversations over the dinner table  or becoming familiar with the language  of that job and I guess kids wanting to  pursue what they know and what they know  is their parents occupations they're a  lot more likely to end up following a  similar path but I yet this makes some  occupations feel really sort of  exclusive to people that are on the  outside that people to people that  aren't from the real with the language  of how that job works and how you get  into it I think it's kind of a problem  for things like academic areas where if  your parents are professors maybe you'll  find it easier to become one too I'm not  sure my parents certainly are didn't  have any scientific background and  neither of them went on to university  but here I am doing a PhD in physics so  I'm certainly not really following in  the footsteps of my parents and I think  there's maybe two sides to it I think  some aspects of being the first in your  family to go to university are a little  bit hard things like just not knowing  how the system works like when I got to  university I had never even heard of a  PhD I had no idea how the progression  from one degree to another worked I had  no idea about how you sort of get a job  in academia I had no idea about even  what you're supposed to do once you get  to uni I've just sort of learnt that as  I've gone I  so certainly didn't know any science  until quite late in my life I was  probably quite a late starter I didn't  really know anything about science until  actually until high school definitely  and then told quite late high school I  didn't have as a kid any of those sort  of science toys that I see now I see  people giving their children these toys  that teach them coding or maths at a  really early age and I just look at that  and I think wow that's crazy like those  exist and I can't imagine what it would  have been like to be exposed to more  scientific ideas at a young age and to  even be exposed to the idea of being a  scientist as a child that would have  been really different for me and I don't  know how it would have changed the  outlook I have now because I think  there's actually benefits for being the  first in your family to do something for  example I've never felt any pressure to  take a certain path I would hate to feel  pressured into taking like it made us an  allure of some certain subject that  traditionally students feel a lot of  pressure from their parents to go into I  would sort of hate that I'm feel very  lucky to just be able to pursue what I  like it's got the challenge of me having  to convince my parents though of what  I'm doing because they don't really have  any idea of the path I'm taking so I  have to sort of explain it to them and  explain maybe why I think it's useful so  that could be hard like sometimes if I  call my mom during like when I've been a  student and I'll tell her that it's  really hard and a stress so she'll just  say why don't you drop out already  yeah are you gonna quit physics yet have  you had enough of it  and I just say no I'm gonna I'm gonna  stick in for a bit longer see how it  goes but I don't really have that person  that's been through it telling me you  know it'll get better it'll it'll be  this will be that so I've just got to  figure that out as I go I think through  this my parents are really just learnt  to trust me  to trust that I'm doing something that I  enjoy and and they're fine with that so  they've been supportive and that's great  one big benefit maybe that comes from  being sort of the first of my family to  be at uni is that I feel like I'm able  to think more outside of the box because  I have no idea what the box is so like  for example I've always done a lot of  sort of extra side projects or  extracurricular activities while  studying because I enjoy doing other  things and I never really had any idea  that I shouldn't be doing something else  whether it's making videos or doing  other science communication or going  into like business startup competitions  or anything else I feel like I feel like  maybe I wouldn't have done so many extra  things if I was trying to follow in  somebody else's footsteps if I wasn't  just walking my own path so to say when  I hear of people whose parents are both  professors and they're on track to be  one themselves I think wow that must be  such a different way of life to the one  I've experienced but I don't think there  is any real good or bad about being on  either side of this I think you still  need to form your own path regardless of  what your parents have done and if  they're an inspiration for you then  that's great  and if you want to do something totally  different from them I think I'd  encourage that as well
 

